# HOME — What openOODA does

File: wiki/Home.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://wiki.openooda.org

## 1. In one minute

openOODA is a systems language. You write `.oo` files.
A systems language talks to the machine: files, processes, and the network.

Effects need a capability token.
A capability token is a permission the function must hold before it can do that effect.
No token, no effect.

The product path is:

1. Typecheck the program.
2. Emit C.
3. Compile that C with host `gcc`.
4. Run the binary.

That path is `ooda run`.

This wiki: https://wiki.openooda.org
Site: https://openooda.org
Source of the pages: https://github.com/openOODA/wiki

## 2. What you can do with it

- Write a program in `.oo` and typecheck it (`ooda check`).
- Build and run it on the native C path (`ooda build`, `ooda run`).
- Run `verify` blocks and asserts (`ooda test`). `ooda run` does not run those blocks.
- Ask the compiler to patch common errors (`ooda fix`).
- Replace one function body from a file (`ooda patch`).
- Talk to an editor through `ooda lsp`.
- Emit other backends when you ask: LLVM IR, WebAssembly, standalone x86_64.

The compiler and the `ooda` command live in the `ooda` tree.
The small standard library that ships with the compiler is `ooda/std`.

## 3. What a program looks like

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

`pub fn` is a public function.
`verify` is a test block next to the function.
`ooda test` runs `verify`. `ooda run` prints the greeting and skips `verify`.

